  bootwright / destroy
      Remove what this context's apply owns, whether that apply completed or not, proving each
      removal before it registers; a failed destroy is replaced by a fresh removal. Follow the
      plan's Stop first and Stop after online cleanup lists (bootwright machine stop): online
      cleanup, such as storage cleanup or a subscription unregistration, needs its Machines
      running, and destroy pauses for their stop after it. A plan that deletes disks needs
      --authorize data-loss. A Machine whose subscription registration cannot be read and that
      you unregistered with the subscription service's own tools is named with --unregistered.
      Review the plan before confirming; --yes skips ordinary confirmation.

  Usage
    bootwright destroy [flags]

  Command flags
      --authorize     Acknowledge a planned risk (data-loss) (values: data-loss)
      --unregistered  Name a Machine whose subscription registration you removed with the
                      subscription service's own tools
      --yes           Confirm the command's ordinary confirmation

  Common flags
      --ascii       Use ASCII status markers
      --color       Select terminal colors (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --context     Select a context (default: current context)
      --detail      Select human detail (values: compact, full) · default compact
      -h, --help    Show help for this command
      -o, --output  Select output mode (values: text, json, yaml) · default text
      --progress    Select progress animation (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --width       Set presentation width (0: automatic) · default 0

  More detail
    Use --help --detail full to include SSH overrides.

  Examples
    bootwright destroy --context lab
